{"authored" [{"question" "q-350830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "d66ab49cfbf4bb4b39421bffaad0f0d04c5f3f720b25be8a87396f615086604a" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-350830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4a}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{-4x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{-4x}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4x}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/ade58ce56684420fb0f00236af50bbb54f76801fc8870e1cce135cbd51f1b726.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4a}) \\\\  & =10(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{4}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "d66ab49cfbf4bb4b39421bffaad0f0d04c5f3f720b25be8a87396f615086604a" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-350830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "abaa9270682b58d8b9e9a28aaa831c88fe855525b815e382ccfdc43dfe25035a" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-350830" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "d66ab49cfbf4bb4b39421bffaad0f0d04c5f3f720b25be8a87396f615086604a" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-350830" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5e^4}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "d66ab49cfbf4bb4b39421bffaad0f0d04c5f3f720b25be8a87396f615086604a" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "d66ab49cfbf4bb4b39421bffaad0f0d04c5f3f720b25be8a87396f615086604a" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({5}^{1}-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/06c51ecc423683f4377248004b7a318f1348836bd752a7f65f94457d1774e64d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "d66ab49cfbf4bb4b39421bffaad0f0d04c5f3f720b25be8a87396f615086604a" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "abaa9270682b58d8b9e9a28aaa831c88fe855525b815e382ccfdc43dfe25035a" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "d66ab49cfbf4bb4b39421bffaad0f0d04c5f3f720b25be8a87396f615086604a" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "d66ab49cfbf4bb4b39421bffaad0f0d04c5f3f720b25be8a87396f615086604a" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "d66ab49cfbf4bb4b39421bffaad0f0d04c5f3f720b25be8a87396f615086604a" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "d66ab49cfbf4bb4b39421bffaad0f0d04c5f3f720b25be8a87396f615086604a" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "d66ab49cfbf4bb4b39421bffaad0f0d04c5f3f720b25be8a87396f615086604a" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "d66ab49cfbf4bb4b39421bffaad0f0d04c5f3f720b25be8a87396f615086604a" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "d66ab49cfbf4bb4b39421bffaad0f0d04c5f3f720b25be8a87396f615086604a" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^\\infty   e^{-3x}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/example-10554.json" "file_sha256" "a4b7998581fef8b80a92bf1d79b4ae0fec79ca69a99fbe4506043f668d4ad30c" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int e^{px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1p e^{px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\,]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-3x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=e^{-3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} e^{-3x} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^{-3x} =0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/bc4ffc41105491f2bd6b37b2482afc64946d8f67f6db83dfa322c400ae4bc2b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (0-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/example-10554.json" "file_sha256" "a4b7998581fef8b80a92bf1d79b4ae0fec79ca69a99fbe4506043f668d4ad30c" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}  3\\cdot 2^{2x+1}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/example-10764.json" "file_sha256" "8ac2b85adcb57ae8f2c3d70ca3bd248242564bfea3185ab496ccfcdc58425411" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int b^{\\,px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1{p\\ln(b)} b^{\\,px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{2x+1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{2x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{0}-{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=2^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} 2^{2x} = 0$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} 2^{2x} = \\infty$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/009f467677932d0ac17e1632c31f4d3aa5271ffe332730d9670e69862b243bef.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/example-10764.json" "file_sha256" "8ac2b85adcb57ae8f2c3d70ca3bd248242564bfea3185ab496ccfcdc58425411" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty  x^3\\cdot 2^{-x^4}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/example-10553.json" "file_sha256" "a8b73808a15e1cb7a9cd70ced2fd54a137d75761ce0fa4faa15baba6b373a9ff" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=x^4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4x^3\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac 14 \\textrm{d}u =x^3\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\dfrac{1}{2^x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = 0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/1969145d74662468ec23d458ac1ad0194d51cbf6b026718b7232466e9357efdf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/example-10553.json" "file_sha256" "a8b73808a15e1cb7a9cd70ced2fd54a137d75761ce0fa4faa15baba6b373a9ff" "basis" 1336 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-350800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}7{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-350800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}7{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =7\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-3}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{-3} \\\\  & =7\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-3}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/4af4a2b7389d3773cc71e843190c6ab111355248a15e6165a8e39e37252061b9.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}7{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =7(\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-350800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a15390178eee87ca05d2f3502c32a44baa8c6e4266127e91909847ae46cbc72"} {"question" "q-350800" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-350800" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7e^{-3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-350809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}6{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-350809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}6{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2a}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{2x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/61a2aafc6dadee9e392e098f77839fdd99f3bb5d30c62cf06fef1be08c4b2682.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}6{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2a}) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-350809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a15390178eee87ca05d2f3502c32a44baa8c6e4266127e91909847ae46cbc72"} {"question" "q-350809" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-350809" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3e^{-2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-100378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-100378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}2{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{3a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{3x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{3x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{3x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/45e8ddd8ddb33dc8d6a701ed75878271b331ce6b3591ba8fae9f4fa49020c12b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{3a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-100378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a15390178eee87ca05d2f3502c32a44baa8c6e4266127e91909847ae46cbc72"} {"question" "q-100378" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-100378" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-100378" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{3}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{3}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/115e3198226940a76b770f5942bb06e6496bd234d6cb5fee2d33f19038931bd3.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a15390178eee87ca05d2f3502c32a44baa8c6e4266127e91909847ae46cbc72"} {"question" "q-152879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{e}^{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2{e}^{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3e}"] ["math" "\\frac{3e}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{3}}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{e}^{3}}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-350830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-350830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4a}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{-4x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{-4x}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4x}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/ade58ce56684420fb0f00236af50bbb54f76801fc8870e1cce135cbd51f1b726.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4a}) \\\\  & =10(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{4}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-350830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a15390178eee87ca05d2f3502c32a44baa8c6e4266127e91909847ae46cbc72"} {"question" "q-350830" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-350830" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5e^4}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({5}^{1}-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/06c51ecc423683f4377248004b7a318f1348836bd752a7f65f94457d1774e64d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a15390178eee87ca05d2f3502c32a44baa8c6e4266127e91909847ae46cbc72"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-346615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-346615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(5-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/83f7d663fa66184b56c4f79d61f6e17012714a3de7512202b3022e91d4325437.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-346615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a15390178eee87ca05d2f3502c32a44baa8c6e4266127e91909847ae46cbc72"} {"question" "q-346615" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-346615" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{\\ln(5)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-351416" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-351416" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{3x}}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{3x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(8-{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/c2af905a46cea7b7512a727a5e53a2a178e4902f7114f86439365684e6685efa.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-351416" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a15390178eee87ca05d2f3502c32a44baa8c6e4266127e91909847ae46cbc72"} {"question" "q-351416" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-351416" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{3\\ln(2)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}4\\cdot {6}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}4\\cdot {6}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}4\\cdot {6}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{6}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{6}^{x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({6}^{0}-{6}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{6}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={6}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{6}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{6}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/2ebee84cf543b25e56b360f145459696554a3b67daaf64c21812cb4b92fa420a.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}4\\cdot {6}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{6}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a15390178eee87ca05d2f3502c32a44baa8c6e4266127e91909847ae46cbc72"} {"question" "q-152876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}"] ["math" "\\frac{24}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-351320" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-351320" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{3\\cdot {4}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[3\\cdot {4}^{x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(3-3\\cdot {4}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }3\\cdot {4}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={4}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{4}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{4}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/fd4020291b348d130fc34969d72ff683aab5b6fb95ea5f7d32b90e5245d56d9c.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }3\\cdot {4}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-351320" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a15390178eee87ca05d2f3502c32a44baa8c6e4266127e91909847ae46cbc72"} {"question" "q-351320" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-351320" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{\\ln(4)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=2+{x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2+{x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[{e}^{u}]_{a}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{3}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/3df1853e93a5ab2a1c1ca4114d450b29a69223bd576b059d9aef1b17c68fe5b7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a15390178eee87ca05d2f3502c32a44baa8c6e4266127e91909847ae46cbc72"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3{e}^{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2{e}^{2}}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}"] ["math" "{e}^{2}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-100424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-100424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u={x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-{e}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/4844d6220d04a6a1fffdd5a485a9e6724aa6065397b8c3a009157a7a2dafc3cf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-100424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a15390178eee87ca05d2f3502c32a44baa8c6e4266127e91909847ae46cbc72"} {"question" "q-100424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-100424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-100424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-100494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-100494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[{2}^{u}]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{a},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{2}^{a}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/b9a29cbd8a029e0f26e3da6a042ada0f8a809053233979bb4ff73db3dcf0f10d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-100494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a15390178eee87ca05d2f3502c32a44baa8c6e4266127e91909847ae46cbc72"} {"question" "q-100494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-100494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-100494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-351529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-351529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to {0}^{-}$ and $u\\to 0$ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\frac{1}{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{e}^{5u}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{5u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{5u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{e}^{5u}}{5}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{e}^{5a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{5a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{5x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{5x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{5x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/2a24352ca6421b3aa61f3f29e3c19373cca011feafce263775a2f692f67ad36b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{5a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-351529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a15390178eee87ca05d2f3502c32a44baa8c6e4266127e91909847ae46cbc72"} {"question" "q-351529" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-351529" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152257" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{1-x}}}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152257" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{1-x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{1-x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{1-x}}}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{\\infty }^{1}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{1-x}}}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[-{e}^{-u}]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}}+\\frac{1}{e}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/4ef3194e946aec805866202a5e0ad521291d71b1fabc2df68d6cb1a4d93da42b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{1-x}}}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{e}-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152257" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a15390178eee87ca05d2f3502c32a44baa8c6e4266127e91909847ae46cbc72"} {"question" "q-152257" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152257" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{e}"] ["math" "e"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "q-152257" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{e}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c7f8699a6495cf1b393b396066c3aebf932eae3952a3140555ca716e0ef2f1e"} {"question" "e-10554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^\\infty   e^{-3x}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/example-10554.json" "file_sha256" "4ceab7adbea10920a3a764828778c21e3a219ed512a5cb911e16119198901a38"} {"question" "e-10554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int e^{px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1p e^{px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\,]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-3x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=e^{-3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} e^{-3x} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^{-3x} =0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/bc4ffc41105491f2bd6b37b2482afc64946d8f67f6db83dfa322c400ae4bc2b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (0-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/example-10554.json" "file_sha256" "4ceab7adbea10920a3a764828778c21e3a219ed512a5cb911e16119198901a38"} {"question" "e-10764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}  3\\cdot 2^{2x+1}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/example-10764.json" "file_sha256" "774e5a27591dd17e29660305ccf486074ae0c38eee607c0ca0e4596c027f3e02"} {"question" "e-10764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int b^{\\,px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1{p\\ln(b)} b^{\\,px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{2x+1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{2x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{0}-{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=2^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} 2^{2x} = 0$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} 2^{2x} = \\infty$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/009f467677932d0ac17e1632c31f4d3aa5271ffe332730d9670e69862b243bef.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/example-10764.json" "file_sha256" "774e5a27591dd17e29660305ccf486074ae0c38eee607c0ca0e4596c027f3e02"} {"question" "e-10553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty  x^3\\cdot 2^{-x^4}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/example-10553.json" "file_sha256" "688979b59094104ff092bd09c8e585129a32bb58ea9306928e901e74947bf438"} {"question" "e-10553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=x^4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4x^3\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac 14 \\textrm{d}u =x^3\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\dfrac{1}{2^x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = 0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/1969145d74662468ec23d458ac1ad0194d51cbf6b026718b7232466e9357efdf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/example-10553.json" "file_sha256" "688979b59094104ff092bd09c8e585129a32bb58ea9306928e901e74947bf438"}] "usage" {"q-100494" [] "e-10554" [] "q-152875" [] "e-10764" [] "q-350830" [] "e-10553" []} "basis" 1567 "retractions" [[17592186301343 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4a}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{-4x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{-4x}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4x}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/ade58ce56684420fb0f00236af50bbb54f76801fc8870e1cce135cbd51f1b726.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4a}) \\\\  & =10(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{4}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186301311 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({5}^{1}-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/06c51ecc423683f4377248004b7a318f1348836bd752a7f65f94457d1774e64d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192764 :question/problem "What is $∫_{- ∞}^{0}x^{2} \\cdot 2^{x^{3}}dx$?"] [17592186192257 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int e^{px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1p e^{px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\,]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-3x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=e^{-3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} e^{-3x} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^{-3x} =0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/bc4ffc41105491f2bd6b37b2482afc64946d8f67f6db83dfa322c400ae4bc2b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (0-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192292 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int b^{\\,px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1{p\\ln(b)} b^{\\,px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{2x+1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{2x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{0}-{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=2^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} 2^{2x} = 0$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} 2^{2x} = \\infty$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/009f467677932d0ac17e1632c31f4d3aa5271ffe332730d9670e69862b243bef.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192256 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x^4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4x^3\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac 14 \\textrm{d}u =x^3\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\dfrac{1}{2^x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = 0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/1969145d74662468ec23d458ac1ad0194d51cbf6b026718b7232466e9357efdf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186189434 17592186189435 17592186189436 17592186189437 17592186189438 17592186301313 17592186301314 17592186301315 17592186301316 17592186301317 17592186301345] "immutable_fields" [[17592186189433 :answer-field/correct] [17592186189433 :answer-field/id] [17592186189433 :answer-field/key] [17592186189433 :answer-field/type] [17592186301312 :answer-field/correct] [17592186301312 :answer-field/id] [17592186301312 :answer-field/key] [17592186301312 :answer-field/type] [17592186301344 :answer-field/correct] [17592186301344 :answer-field/id] [17592186301344 :answer-field/key] [17592186301344 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
